WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
1234

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.980
Scientists have long wondered

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.460
why humans like music and why we're so good

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:14.749
at making it.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.240
Our love affair has deep roots

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.620
even our closest primate cousins

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.180
have been seen banging out riffs in the wild

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.699
but what makes music... MUSIC?

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.620
Scientists have identified 18 so-called

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.370
music universals.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.980
Six of them are related to rhythm.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:35.880
They include:

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.289
The universals came from 304 recordings of

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.529
music around the world

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.360
But do they hold up

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.420
when people invent music from scratch?

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.500
To find out scientists asked

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.800
non-musicians to play a game of telephone.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.000
The first person tried to imitate random

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.300
drumming noise from a computer.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.340
The second person tried to imitate the first.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.900
By the time the final person laid down

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.120
their riffs something amazing happened.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.700
The random beats had transformed into

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.200
easy-to-learn highly structured patterns

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.900
that reflected all six universals.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.290
This, says scientists, might not explain

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.040
why we like music.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.700
It might help explain

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.100
how our brains are wired to produce it.

